# Children of the Five Winds — The Unicorn Clan

**Version:** 0.4.1
**Source:** Legend of the Five Rings: Children of the Five Winds
**Publisher:** Fantasy Flight Games

---

## The Unicorn Clan

If one were to consult the Imperial records from the dawn of the Empire, there would be no mention of a Unicorn Clan. The Imperial archives would, however, contain carefully preserved letters and records that document the worry that Hantei expressed to his other Divine Siblings regarding their lost sister, Shinjo.

When Hantei gave each of his siblings a role in shaping Rokugan in the early days, he paused when he turned his gaze to Shinjo. Before he could speak, his fearless and passionate sister asked to leave the boundaries of their fledgling empire and explore the world beyond. Shinjo and her Ki-Rin Clan would leave, learn, and return to Rokugan with their newfound knowledge of foreign lands and the peoples and other entities that resided there. Hantei, believing in the abilities of his sister and the benefit of her quest, agreed. But for nearly eight hundred years, the clan did not return. For a time, their absence was mourned, but as the generations passed, the memory of the Ki-Rin was left as little more than a footnote in history—until a new clan, one changed through heat, battle, and time, came thundering up to the Carpenter Wall. There stood the descendants of explorers, rogues, diplomats, and nomadic warriors. They were forgotten strangers, presumed lost, returned home. And when they returned, the Unicorn Clan rocked the foundation of the Empire.

### The Divine Sibling Shinjo

Shinjo is documented as a force to be reckoned with, even when compared to the other Divine Siblings. The words tenacious, curious, compassionate, thoughtful, and fierce are just a few used to describe her. She rejected the idea that divine blood made her or her siblings superior to regular people, and she dedicated her life to both protecting her followers and doing what she could to see them flourish.

Of all the Divine Siblings, it was Shinjo who was noted as being the most in awe of the world around her. She rarely stayed in one place for long, preferring to explore the Mortal Realm. In a letter supposedly written by Togashi, he described Shinjo: "My sister was the most changed by our coming to the Mortal Realm. In that moment, her veins became the rivers of the world, her heart was filled with the voices of all peoples, and her breath became the breeze. She greets every sunrise with a smile, thankful to our mother that such a treasure as this world exists."

### The Lost Sibling and Shinjo's Departure

When the missing Divine Sibling, Fu Leng, rose up from a pit in the south and sought revenge for having been abandoned, Shinjo stood before him and tried to speak with him. His rage had consumed him, and he lashed out at the siblings—he gravely injured Hida, challenged Hantei for the throne, and even murdered one of Doji's children. Even then, Shinjo could not turn away from her brother, and the strife among her kin was a deep pain in her heart.

After the Day of Thunder, uncertainty overcame Shinjo. She blamed herself for Fu Leng's death, believing that if she had tried a little harder, she could have gotten through to him. Hantei saw her suffering, and when she asked to leave Rokugan to map the outside world in order to better prepare the Empire for what might lie beyond the borders of its domain, he agreed. Though she released her vassals from their service to her, many chose to remain at her side and travel into the unknown with her. Among them were the sage Iuchi, the humble trader Ide, and Utaku Shiko, the righteous daughter of her most loyal follower. Those who chose to stay behind were tasked by Shinjo with recording the history of the Empire so that when the Ki-Rin returned, they would know what had happened in their absence. Those who fulfilled that promise would one day be honored as a Minor Clan, known as the Fox.

As Shinjo departed, Doji met her at the western border, giving her a sandalwood fan she had carved herself so that no matter how far she roamed, Doji's sister would never forget her family back home. Shinjo was said to have truly treasured this gift, and she kept it close to her at all times. Determined, Shinjo vanished across the Plains of Wind and Stone, never to be seen again.

Two generations after she left, the blessed mirror Lady Shinjo had left behind suddenly went dark, unreflecting as stone. The Ki-Rin star vanished in the sky. With the Seppun interpreting these as ill omens, the venerable Emperor Genji declared a festival in his aunt's memory, and the Empire mourned the loss of the Ki-Rin.

### INTO THE UNKNOWN

The Ki-Rin's journey almost ended in disaster, scarcely one hundred li beyond their own borders, when they found themselves wandering the desolate and punishing Plains of Wind and Stone. This desert was brutally cold in winter and perishingly hot in summer, with so little water and food that the Ki-Rin began to starve.

To make matters worse, they were soon attacked by a band of horseback-riding nomads known as Ujik. Although the courage and skill of Shinjo's samurai were second to none, doing battle with such a mobile and flexible enemy seemed impossible until Shinjo and Utaku Shiko captured a handful of Ujik horses and adopted Ujik tactics.

Impressed by the prowess of Shinjo's people, the Ujik halted their raids and began to trade in peace with Ide and the rest of the clan. With the addition of Ujik horses and by adopting Ujik nomadic practices and herding techniques, the Ki-Rin soon were no longer starving and began to thrive. In time, many of the Ujik joined the clan, binding themselves through promises of fellowship and a number of strategic marriages. These Ujik, now called the Moto, soon became a vital part of the Ki-Rin Clan, and the friendship of the Ki-Rin allowed the Ujik to press back against their traditional enemies, human and otherwise, at the limits of their territory.

The Ki-Rin spent perhaps as much as one hundred years living among the Ujik. By the time they were ready to move on, every single Ki-Rin was mounted atop a sturdy Ujik desert horse, and the entire clan had adopted the Ujik saddle and, even more importantly, the stirrup. Now, when they made war, Shinjo's people fought from horseback.

#### Shinjo's Captivity

During their conflict with the Ujik, Ki-Rin scouts and explorers had been hard at work probing the limits of the unassailable mountains to the south and the trackless deserts to the west, the so-called Burning Sands. In time, the Ki-Rin Clan saw no other course and rode west once more. Traveling across the Burning Sands was the greatest challenge yet faced by the Ki-Rin, but when they reached the lush river valleys of the Cradle of the World, they were rewarded with splendor and gifts such as they could scarcely believe. The pharaohs of Rempet claimed divine lineage from the Sun God, Shem, and they declared Shinjo-no-Kami to be their sister from afar. She and her followers were lavished with riches by the sorcerer-king Nephrentep. Half-dead with hunger and thirst, the Ki-Rin eagerly accepted the hospitality of Rempet, even as Shinjo and some of the elders of the clan were troubled that the city's priests and nobles openly practiced enslavement.

Nephrentep's hospitality was a ruse. As soon as the Ki-Rin let down their guard, the pharaoh struck, snatching Shinjo from among them and spiriting her away to his temple-palace. The sorcerer-king's undying guard attacked the Ki-Rin and drove them into hiding. Iuchi declared that the sinister priests of Rempet were blood sorcerers and that they intended to use Shinjo-no-Kami's divine blood in a ritual to prolong Nephrentep's life. The Ki-Rin all agreed that they would die before they allowed such a fate to befall their beloved Shinjo. Clever Ide found allies in Rempet: al-Qamari, a secret organization devoted to opposing the Sun Kings. Together, the Ki-Rin and al-Qamari staged a daring attack on Nephrentep's temple-palace. The attack seemed doomed to failure, as the pharaoh's magic and his undying guard were too strong for the rescuers until the gods themselves intervened: the moon blotted out the sun, throwing day into night. The moon ruled supreme until the wicked king was cast down and Shinjo was liberated. Once freed, Shinjo united the Ki-Rin Clan and the oppressed peoples of Rempet to destroy the corrupt empire of the sorcerer-kings.

#### A Journey out of Knowledge

Not long after destroying the Empire of Rempet, Shinjo gathered her clan to her and pronounced that they would go their separate ways. "The world is too big," she said, "for those on any one journey to ever discover it all." She divided the Ki-Rin Clan into five hordes and sent them each in a different direction. Shinjo took her Blue Horde west. Iuchi took his Green Horde south. Moto Chabi Khan took her White Horde north, continuing the journey across the Burning Sands. Utaku Shiko took her Purple Horde east into the Ivory Kingdoms. And Ide's Golden Horde remained in place, exploring the coast of the Conqueror's Sea. The accounts brought back by the three hordes returning to Rokugan—now calling themselves the Unicorn Clan—are sometimes unbelievable. They told of empires of snakelike beings; raiders with the body of a horse and the torso of a human; giants with only one eye; cursed pits and blessed valleys; and the Unicorn itself, a horselike spirit of water and fire that some say fathered Shinjo's children. Tragically, Iuchi's Green Horde never returned. According to rumor, they still ride the world, seeking its uttermost limits.

### The Ride through Shadow

At length, the time came to return to Rokugan. Rather than cross the Burning Sands, the Unicorn rode south of the Pillar of the Sky mountains into the tangled forests and steep valleys of the Ivory Kingdoms. Although now mounted on the finest horses in the world, the Unicorn struggled to make good time, as they were frequently turned back by the people and creatures they encountered among the hills and valleys. When they reached the eastern edge of the Ivory Kingdoms, they found one more barrier in their path: the twisted, unnatural forest of the western Shadowlands.

According to the Ide chroniclers, it took the Unicorn Clan forty years to cross from the Conqueror's Sea to the Kaiu Wall in Rokugan. It seems an improbable duration for a journey that can now be made along the Sand Road in a handful of months, but the modern route does not account for the Shadowlands. In that cursed place, life and death, and even time itself, do not have the same meaning they do in the rest of the world.

Who can say how long the Unicorn rode and fought their way through those blighted lands? Surely, they proceeded with the same mix of boldness and caution that had guided them across the Burning Sands, stopping in places of relative safety and sending forth their scouts before plunging headlong into danger. The Ide Chronicles are difficult to reconcile with the Imperial Histories in this period.

### THE UNICORN'S RETURN

In the year 815, the ground beyond the Carpenter Wall shook with the hoofbeats of warhorses.

The horde that rode across the Shadowlands did not resemble the legends of the tiny clan that had departed the Empire so long ago. What stood before the Empire now was a group of outsiders, nearly unrecognizable. The Crab Clan had learned not to trust anything that came from the south, and they rebuffed all attempts to parley. Frustrated by the stubborn rejection, the Khan of Khans, Shinjo Nishijin, led a decisive

attack at the Battle of Burning Shadows, utilizing Iuchi meishōdō (name magic) techniques to blast through the Wall and into Rokugan.

Word of the invading hordes mobilized the other clans against the invaders. But even the combined efforts of the Lion, Scorpion, and Crab could not halt the horde's advance. The Empire had never before encountered such large, powerful horses or warriors so adept at fighting from the massive beasts' backs. The enemy was too mobile, too strong, and it employed tactics that had never been seen before by the Great Clans' generals. Many feared that the invaders would ride to the capital and usurp the throne. But the horde passed the Imperial City and rode onward to the north, near the lands of the Lion and Phoenix.

As the clans fought the invaders, the horde dispatched a group of diplomats, including Shinjo Nishijin himself, to approach their allies in the Crane Clan in secret. Although initially skeptical, Nishijin stunned the Crane Clan Champion, Doji Ryobu, when he presented the very sandalwood fan given to Shinjo by her sister. Scholars from the Miya, Asahina, and Asako determined the fan was authentic, and as a result, Nishijin and his diplomats were presented to the Emperor. The Fox Clan Champion, whose clan was descended from those who had stayed behind when Shinjo had left centuries before, greeted Nishijin and his people as one would a cousin. With endorsements from the Crane and the Fox, the Imperial Court acknowledged the Unicorn as the successors of the Ki-Rin, and an Imperial edict was drafted just in time to prevent a pitched battle between the Ki-Rin and the Lion. During that year's Winter Court, the Crane Champion embraced Nishijin and called him brother. Thus, the Unicorn Clan was restored to their lands and to their rightful place in Rokugan.

### CHILDREN OF THE FIVE WINDS

While the Unicorn Clan—known to some as the Children of the Five Winds, which represents their five main families—are recognized as the Ki-Rin's descendants, there are many in Rokugan who hold reservations about the clan that returned, and many have never fully accepted the Unicorn as true members of the Empire. The Lion resent the loss of their ancestral territories, many associating the Unicorn's return with the decline of the Lion's golden age. The Scorpion have never forgotten how the Unicorn disrupted the political balance and their plans that had been in motion for generations. To the other clans, the Unicorn's ways are irreconcilable with the rest of the Empire's. They regard the Unicorn as disruptive and strange, and the Unicorn's foreign mannerisms make many Rokugani uncomfortable. The Unicorn have resisted acknowledging any Fortunes who became established after the Ki-Rin left. Centuries of isolation and the fear of change have left the Unicorn strangers in their own homeland.

Even though the Unicorn still face discrimination and ostracization to this day, the Empire has embraced aspects of their culture and has benefited from their expansive knowledge of the world. From beyond its borders, new crops, cultivars, innovations, and inventions have spread across the Emerald Empire. Much of what Rokugan knows about the outside world is from Unicorn scholars and documents, and the Empire has been made stronger by the introduction of heartier horses and ponies, as well as by expansive cavalry and anticavalry tactics.

Wandering the northwestern lands and watching over the western border, the Unicorn Clan refuse to compromise their way of life for the mere comfort of those who do not bother to know them. Resisting what others want them to be, the Children of the Five Winds seek their place among the Great Clans of Rokugan.

## Families of the Unicorn

While often seen as misfits among the other Great Clans, the people of the Unicorn Clan are dedicated to walking the fine line between adapting to their circumstances and being true to themselves. They are proudly wild people, untamed and unbroken, but not uncivilized. Indeed, they have centuries of practice at living among those who regard them as outsiders and at breaking down those barriers through patience and dedication. The others may raise their eyebrows at the "western-border misfits," but the families of the Unicorn have earned their place among the Great Clans, and they have proven themselves time and time again. To their mind, it is only a matter of time before they are fully embraced. And if that day never comes, then so be it. Lady Shinjo never required the approval of others; neither do her children.

While seemingly destined to be strangers wherever they go, this has only strengthened the bonds between the Unicorn families. The Ide present themselves as the "face" of the clan, while the Utaku and Moto defend it as its left and right hands. The Iuchi advise and uncover threats, while the Shinjo push the clan ever forward. Led by the Khan of Khans, who is traditionally the Shinjo daimyō, the families work together to find a place in the ever-changing world.

### FIVE WINDS, ONE CLAN

Over time, Shinjo's tribe grew, and all the peoples who swore her fealty became a single clan with diverse views. It was Shinjo's hope that, with time, they would come to see one another as family. To this end, she allowed those who swore into her service to keep their traditions, but she also made them swear to abide by a number of decrees. These laws are still followed by all the Unicorn families centuries later:

- Similar to her brother Akodo, Lady Shinjo taught that service was not an obligation but a choice. The warrior chose who they would fight and die for. Unicorn Clan children do not inherit their parents' titles or positions. Instead, when they come of age, they are offered a choice: become a clan samurai, with all the connected rights and obligations, or leave the clan and live as a peasant. No shame befalls any who leave, but those who remain undergo the coming-of-age ceremony to become clan samurai.
- Rōnin, other clan samurai, peasants, and even non-Rokugani may join the Unicorn if they can prove their loyalty and worth. Lady Shinjo created a specific process for outsiders to come into the fold, and these tests are still used by the clan today.
- To draw sword against kin was one of the highest crimes among the Ki-Rin, and Lady Shinjo forbade duels between her samurai. This order is interpreted to mean that jousting, iaijutsu duels, and other forms of ritualized combat are forbidden between Unicorn Clan samurai. Instead, they settle issues of honor with non-lethal contests, such as horse archery against targets, or races. If Imperial law or other circumstance absolutely requires a duel between Unicorn, then it is conducted with wooden weapons, and only to first strike.
Apart from laws, there are also some ingrained Unicorn customs that derive from centuries of tradition:

#### The Shinjo Family Mon

### SHINJO

The earliest accounts of Shinjo describe her from afar: a mysterious woman with an otherworldly presence, chasing after a chimeric being that galloped on air. She swiftly became a legend throughout the northern lands. No one knew who she was or why she chased this strange creature, a combination of deer and dragon with a flaming mane. According to legend, the first mortal to ever approach her was the hermit woman Utaku. After two years of seeking the legendary ki-rin together, they both approached civilization for the first time, side by side.

Shinjo fell in love with the world from afar, gazing at its wonders through the heavenly window of her childhood home among the stars. A fallen goddess with endless curiosity, she longed to explore the world from corner to corner. She was bright-eyed, fearless, and impetuous. Animals flocked to her. Wild ponies seemed to invite her onto their backs. She never spoke down to the mortals she encountered, nor did she try to change or conquer them. She wanted only to understand, to discover and chart as much as possible before moving on. Others joined in her journeys thundering across the lands, and when she finally reunited with her siblings, Lady Shinjo had acquired a devoted following, hundreds of diverse peoples united by their sense of adventure.

> **BLUE HORDE**
>
> After destroying the Empire of Rempet, Shinjo divided her clan into five hordes and sent them to explore the world. Shinjo herself led the Blue Horde west, seeking the edge of the Mortal Realm. When the horde returned centuries later, Shinjo was not among them.

The Shinjo family is the living legacy of Lady Shinjo herself. While centuries of travel have changed them, they have never forgotten the things their founder fought for, and they remain devoted to living by her example. With an open and just heart, and a love for discovery, the Shinjo have made themselves into the ribbon that binds the Unicorn families together.

The Shinjo are an unabashed people who wear their hearts on their sleeves, romantics who tend to follow their whims and seek to wring the absolute most they can out of life. They see a world filled with challenges to be overcome and secrets to be revealed. While they are the leaders of the clan, they also value independence and freedom from obligation. This is why, even when approaching the families that have sworn them fealty, they ask instead of ordering. This reputation has not always served the Shinjo well. Because they are free spirits, they are viewed as capricious. Because they value self-determination, they are seen as undisciplined or as weak leaders. Many assume that a Shinjo cannot be counted upon, that they break vows, that they cannot see the greater good beyond their own horse's snout. Other clans look upon Unicorn territory and see provinces that are overgrown, unruly, and wild. And perhaps those lands are a little chaotic. The truth is that the Shinjo prefer some excitement in their lives. Peace comes to the Shinjo not in moments of stillness, but in the very midst of the storm. They value the ability to test themselves and their own mettle. How else is one to discover the things of which one is capable, or what life has to teach?

> **DISCOVERING MORE**
>
> Discover more about the Shrine of the Ki-Rin in Emerald Empire: The Essential Guide to Rokugan, pages 156–157.

This may be why Shinjo lords are notoriously difficult to find. Rarely dwelling at their estates, they instead spend weeks roaming the countryside seeking adventure. Even the family daimyō, Lady Altansarnai, the Khan of Khans herself, disappears for weeks at a time to go hunting and riding in the wilds with her family. Wanderlust is a trait the Shinjo have embraced; always seeking new experiences, they revel in the thrill of discovery.

While generally misunderstood, the Shinjo enjoy good relations with many clan families. The Doji in particular often champion the Shinjo's causes, and in spite of their vast differences, the two families remain as close as their founders were. Many in the Doji are fascinated by Shinjo culture, and several Crane nobles vacation in Unicorn lands, determined to "rough it" as the Shinjo do. The Shinjo also enjoy close ties with the Hiruma and Hida families of the Crab Clan. The Crab have never forgotten their defeat at the Battle of Burning Shadows, but rather than hold a grudge, they remain impressed by the Unicorn's ingenuity and prowess. Both clans share a certain kinship as "outsiders" to Rokugani court culture, and of all the clans, the Crab seem the most open to learning what the Unicorn have to teach. As a result, many Shinjo spend a year or two at the Carpenter Wall, and since the loss of the Hiruma family traditions with the fall of their ancestral keep, Hiruma Scouts often train in Shinjo dōjō. For many years, the Shinjo also enjoyed good relations with the Phoenix Clan. The Shrine of the Ki-Rin on the edge of Phoenix lands is a sacred place to many Unicorn samurai, and in an agreement that is unlike any other among the clans, the Phoenix allow any Unicorn to journey there without travel papers. However, recently the Phoenix seem to be withdrawing their support, and the Shinjo can only guess as to why.

The Shinjo were unrecognizable when they returned to the Empire. They had become nomadic wanderers, incorporating foreign influences into their traditions. Their way of life was decidedly not Rokugani. Yet they had also preserved aspects of Rokugan's early culture that the rest of the Empire had forgotten. When they returned, they brought these lost traditions back with them. So while the Shinjo seemed completely alien to most, historians were quick to point out that these were also the Empire's ways.

> **THE EXPLORERS' REST**
>
> Many believe that the Ki-Rin spent their entire exodus in exploration. But in truth, the Ki-Rin hordes settled down many times in their history, founding independent city-states and even claiming vast territories. For several hundred years, the idea of returning to Rokugan never even occurred to the White and Golden Hordes. They believed their destiny was to create new nations, not to return to their homeland. Only when the hordes reunited, and after a brief power struggle, did the Unicorn decide to relinquish their territories and return to Rokugan. Their abandoned ruins still dot the lands from which they withdrew.

> **SHINJO TRAVEL POETRY**
>
> Departing from typical convention, the Shinjo style of poetry was developed during the Ki-Rin's journeys. Referred to as travel poetry, it is a combination of proverb and poem, usually written to convey a lesson or wry observation. Alliteration is a common device, as is humor, but neither is a hard rule. Most travel poetry also rhymes, although the schemes vary. In fact, many of the Shinjo's individual families claim their own style, unique from any other.

Moving between estates based on season, or camping and hunting in their vast meadows, the Shinjo are a people who never hold still. Wealth is measured by the number and quality of horses and ponies, with most Shinjo keeping a small herd. Large families are common, as is adoption, with five or six children being completely normal. It is also not unusual for Shinjo parents to raise children without ever being "officially" married. Family units with three or more parents, while rare, carry no stigma within the clan. Shinjo family culture instills a strong sense of freedom. Shinjo do not care for being told what to do, and they do not like to be contained. They would rather sleep beneath the stars against the warmth of their steeds than curl up in cramped tents or their own beds. They draw little distinction between "civilization" and "nature," preferring to see themselves as a part of the natural world instead of separate from it. In a similar vein, Shinjo tend to encourage their children to pursue their own interests, granting them a greater say in their own education and training than is typical for would-be samurai. Other clans tend to regard Shinjo children as "wild" and "unrestrained" or even "poorly behaved." But this budding independence and free-spiritedness is seen as virtuous through the eyes of the Shinjo. After all, the world is larger than even most Great Clans know, and it was this eagerness to engage with new ideas that allowed the family to survive centuries among unfamiliar peoples. They may be frequently painted as frivolous or even barbaric, but nothing is further from the truth. It is simply that they tend to feel a certain calling, a tug at the strings of their hearts, continually pulling them toward unexplored horizons. Shinjo herself once said that her purpose was to uncover the hidden threats of the world and shine a light upon them. The Shinjo embody her example and ride toward the unknown.

### Schools and Dōjō

Among the family's oldest and most important traditions is the Shinjo style of horse archery. In the earliest centuries, all samurai were expected to master this skill, regardless of clan or station. Besides being important in warfare, horseback archery duels were also an acceptable alternative to the quick-draw duel. In some provinces, a person could not even call themself a samurai unless they could shoot from horseback; ceremoniously proving they could do this was the precursor to the gempuku coming-of-age ceremony. While time eroded the Rokugani horse archery tradition, this martial skill proved invaluable to the Ki-Rin during their journeys, and as they encountered similar techniques, they were quick to adopt them as well. The Shinjo Mounted Archer School teaches the foremost horseback archery techniques in the Empire. Its reputation is such that students entertain many requests to perform a ritualized form of horse archery known as yabusame at courts and festivals, even from outside the clan. Like the Moto style, Shinjo horse archery makes use of a narrow-grip thumb draw that is aided by a ring instead of the traditional glove-aided "long draw" of other schools. Because shooting requires both hands, the archer must direct their horse using only their lower legs, and they must account for the turbulence of riding full speed in their draw. It is one of the most difficult conditions of archery, which is why the Shinjo horse archery masters are considered some of the best archers in the Empire.

Another of the Shinjo's famous martial schools is the Shinjo Outrider School (see the Legend of the Five Rings roleplaying game core rulebook, page 85). Skilled at reconnaissance and protective escorts, its adherents are second only to the Utaku family in how closely they bond with their horses. Their techniques date back to the Shinjo's exodus in the deserts, and it was the Shinjo Outriders who kept the clan safe from raids and intercepted bandits and other dangers. Members of an offshoot of this school, the Shinjo Cartographers, are considered some of the best mappers in the Emerald Empire; they are often deployed as scouts in addition to aiding architects and surveyors.

A unique magistrate tradition has also taken root among the Shinjo, owing not only to a cultural sense of justice but also to the resurgence of the notorious Forest Killer Bandits. Shinjo magistrates are famous both for tracking down criminals over vast distances and for their unconventional subduing techniques. But they are also known for their mercy and sound judgment, often choosing to overlook minor infractions in the interest of maintaining communal harmony. This is a controversial attitude in the eyes of more stringent organizations, as Shinjo magistrates sometimes skirt the law for what they consider to be the greater good.

Lesser celebrated is the Shinjo's rich history of diplomats and peacemakers, tracing back to the first time Shinjo met the Ujik beneath a flag of peace. Unlike the cerebral and trade-focused Ide, Shinjo diplomats rely on natural charisma and empathy to broker agreements. Many would-be enemies are preemptively disarmed by the Shinjo's friendliness.

> **UNICORN CLAN CUISINE**
>
> The Unicorn claim some of the most unique cuisine in the Empire. This stems not only from their culture but also from the variance in cooking fuel and ingredients available to them. For instance, Unicorn provinces are landlocked; the coastal seafoods that make up most clan diets would take weeks to reach them. While some smaller ocean fish can be transported in barrels, transporting larger or more delicate fish presents challenges, and even preserved fish may spoil before reaching their destination. Fresh bluefin, which can range from eight to fifteen feet long, are transported in massive saltwater casks at great expense, making them a rare Unicorn delicacy.
> For this reason, Unicorn cuisine makes extensive use of freshwater fish, pastoral veggies, and various meadow grains. Indeed, many have observed significant overlap between what the Unicorn eat and what they feed their horses. Unicorn diets also feature game with far more frequency than what is considered typical in Rokugan. Deer, boar, goat, rabbit, and ox meat are all very common, as is the fermentation of milk. This is because the Unicorn rarely observe the Shintao eating taboos, which forbid the consumption of red meat and certain other game.
> While most traditional Rokugani cuisines attempt to elevate the ingredients' natural flavors, often focusing on savory "umami" and sweet profiles, the Unicorn incorporate spicy and sour flavors that are meant to contrast or complement one another. Imported spices are staples of many Unicorn dishes, and most traditional foods are communal in nature. Some Unicorn conventions, such as deep-frying with batter, have proven popular among other clans. This, and the wealth of new fruits, vegetables, and other foods the Unicorn have introduced, have left a lasting impact on the Empire's many cuisines.

### The Lands of the Shinjo

Travelers arriving in Shinjo territory might mistake these vast, quiet plains as abandoned. Entire tracts roll by without sign of village or farm, much less the proud keeps dotting the landscape of other Great Clans. Some may even shake their heads at the virgin land and pristine bamboo groves—precious resources left unused. But these lands are not empty. The villages are mobile, the people nomadic, and the seasonal orchards and farmlands of wild barley and rye hidden in plain sight. The Shinjo could never find peace standing still. With wandering hearts, they roam their lands, ever seeking a new adventure.

This is not to say there are no permanent settlements, just that they are sparse and spread out, with few roads connecting them. Horses are as hard on roads as roads are on horses, so the Shinjo maintain as few roads as necessary.

#### Far Traveler Castle

The seat of the Shinjo's power, Far Traveler Castle, is so named due to the difficult journey required to reach it. It is the northernmost of the Unicorn's palaces and one of the most remote in the Empire, rising over Water Dragon Lake to the west and Dragon's Heart Forest to the south. While most keeps are surrounded by a thriving "castle city," the lands surrounding Far Traveler Castle are sparsely populated, with only a way station village and an ordu's tents pitched nearby.

The castle itself is an unusual triangular fortification, with round towers and white stone, incorporating architectural techniques from beyond Rokugan. Within the utilitarian walls are some of the most magnificent interiors in the Empire, with indoor cascades, vast living quarters, and rich appointments. Among the keep's most famous chambers, the Red Hall contains a collection of hunting trophies and exotic ceremonial weapons, some hung by Unicorn Champions of old. The current champion, Shinjo Altansarnai, proudly hangs monuments to her children's hunting prowess on these walls. With an interior onsen heated with coals and a large courtyard for sports and competitions, the incredible wealth of the Shinjo is on full display.

#### Water Dragon Lake

Located at the foot of Far Traveler Castle, Water Dragon Lake is a subject of legends older than the Empire. The oldest claim that the Water Dragon witnessed one of her kind fall in love with a mortal, giving up her dragon form in order to start a human family. Horrified that this same fate might befall her, the Water Dragon hid her heart at the bottom of the lake so that she could never share it with another. This is how the nearby Dragon's Heart Forest got its name, and it also accounts for Water Dragon Shrine, whose keepers still draw upon the lake's waters for worship. Other tales say that anyone who dives deep enough to touch the bottom of this lake will surface in the Bay of the Sun Goddess, all the way on the eastern coast.

> **Shinjo Rumors**
>
> "The oldest village along Water Dragon Lake not only predates the Empire, but I heard most of the villagers are actually Yobanjin who bargained with Lady Shinjo herself for their self-sovereignty."
> "Did you know Altansarnai's daughter was being considered for a position within the Imperial Guard? Too bad about the falling out with the Lion; there's no way she's going to receive that honor now."

### Shinjo Haruko

The Unicorn Champion's only biological daughter spends most of her days on horseback, galloping along the shore of Water Dragon Lake. Only a few seasons ago, all paths seemed open to her: a likely appointment to the Imperial Guard, fame and recognition in the Bowman's Wager at the Imperial Winter Court, and the many adventures both would bring. However, in the wake of an Imperial censure and the falling out of the Shinjo and Ikoma families, all of those doors have abruptly shut. Shinjo Haruko spends her days riding her favorite horse, Yue, through the surrounding lands, but what she is seeking, a new purpose, cannot be found near Far Traveler Castle.

Haruko is never content to remain in one place. She grew up in many courts, accompanying her mother, and she has friends among virtually all the Great Clans as a consequence. Although she is one of the most talented horse archers of her generation, Haruko has always tended to stir up trouble when bored. And she has become so bored as of late…

#### Horse Girl

Any steed Haruko rides or has personally trained gives the rider an additional bonus success on any Movement action check they make.

#### Wishes at Dawn

In addition to her martial pursuits, Haruko also has a great love for the theater. Her most recent play, Wishes at Dawn, debuted at a provincial Winter Court and was met with significant praise. It depicts two star-crossed lovers, a Kakita and a Matsu, who must cross swords on the battlefield despite their secret love. While it is popular, neither the Matsu nor the Kakita family is particularly amused.

## UTAKU

Most Great Clan families have built a culture centered around the life of their founder. The Utaku family built theirs around two: the Ki-Rin Clan Thunder, Lady Utaku, and her daughter, Utaku Shiko. Yet while there are many accounts from Utaku Shiko's life and adventures, her mother's story remains steeped in legend. These stories describe Utaku as a woman of few words, great convictions, and preternatural fighting prowess. Scholars debate whether she ever spoke at all, but while she never actually swore any oaths to Shinjo, she was one of the Kami's most devoted followers. Legends describe her as a kind, good-humored person who smiled often, but they also mention her great ire against cruelty. This is the version of Utaku remembered in the tales of the Seven Thunders: a warrior who would not hesitate to avenge an injustice, no matter how small—and Shinjo's right-hand enforcer who let her sword do her talking. While only a fraction of the Utaku follow the Battle Maiden tradition, the entire family embraces the values that Utaku and her daughter stood for. They are the warrior conscience of the Unicorn, a reminder that no matter what the future may demand, they must never cast aside the things they truly believe in.

### Reputation and Values

The Utaku are the most celebrated of the Unicorn's great houses, both within and outside of the clan. More reserved than the Shinjo or Moto, less ostentatious than the Ide or Iuchi, and far more insular and traditional than the other Unicorn families, the Utaku strive to live their principles and let their deeds stand for themselves. Embracing a nomadic lifestyle, the Utaku treasure practices that instill a sense of wonder in the natural world. This may be why the Utaku raise some of the Unicorn's greatest literary scholars and calligraphers, and produce some of the Empire's greatest poets.

Yet it is the Utaku warrior traditions and their reverence for Bushidō for which they are most famed, and why the family is universally respected, even among the clan's rivals. To most, the Utaku family is synonymous with the Unicorn's elite warrior tradition: the Shiotome, or "Battle Maidens." Membership among this prestigious sorority is chief among the family's callings, a spiritual aspiration to tread in the footsteps of the family's founders. (For information on the Utaku Battle Maiden School, see the Legend of the Five Rings roleplaying game core rulebook, page 86.) While the Utaku Battle Maiden School is the most prestigious of the Utaku's schools, many other paths are open to Utaku samurai. Horse Lord Disciples are considered Rokugan's greatest equestrian breeders and trainers. Utaku who do not join one of these schools may find a place in any of the other Unicorn schools, and they often need only ask—such is the family's favor and reputation.

> **PURPLE HORDE**
>
> When the Five Winds of the Ki-Rin parted, Utaku Shiko led the Purple Horde east into the Ivory Kingdoms. They were the first to part and the last to return.

### Culture and Traditions

Of all the Unicorn families, the Utaku are the most traditional. After all, their ways were how the Purple Horde survived their travels throughout the Ivory Kingdoms and the haunted Ghostlands. They have embraced Akodo's codified interpretation of Bushidō and built their way of life around horse veneration and fighting injustice. While the Utaku family produces fine courtiers, scholars, and artisans, its people's ways are ultimately martial, and the family is ever ready to protect and serve the Shinjo, just as their founders did. Upon coming of age, an Utaku is expected to make personal vows instead of swearing fealty to a daimyō or champion. Service to one's lord is taken for granted, and oaths of fealty are not necessary. Instead, they often swear vows of silence, poverty, or dietary restriction (usually committing to a pescatarian, vegetarian, or vegan diet). They may also devote themself to a particular cause, such as mastery of a specific martial art or the righting of a specific historical injustice. This honors the family's founders, as both Utaku and her daughter, Shiko, never directly swore to serve Shinjo, but they did so tirelessly without being asked.

> **The Thunders' Daughters**
>
> One of the Battle Maidens' closest interclan allies is the Lion's Pride, the Matsu family's monastic council of elders and elite fighters. Both share similar lifestyles and values, and friendships between the two orders are common. Their mutual respect goes back to the early days of the Empire, as Matsu and Utaku were said to consider each other friendly rivals. Upon the Unicorn's eventual return, the Lion's Pride and Battle Maidens reestablished this bond. In Otosan Uchi, the Battle Maidens and Lion's Pride together maintain a modest dōjō. Known as the House of the Thunders' Daughters, it is a place where young hopefuls of both organizations can train and spar together while sharing in the siblinghood of martial tradition. Older veterans of both the Matsu and Utaku sometimes retire here to train samurai of both traditions in the interest of mutual understanding and respect.

#### Matriarchal Traditions

The most stringent of the Utaku's traditions is its matriarchal structure. According to the family charter, only women are permitted to ride into war. Most influential leadership positions are only open to those who identify as women, if only due to the charter's careful wording. For example, the Utaku family daimyō can only ever be a samurai who has "mastered the ways of Utaku Shiko." This is usually interpreted to mean the Utaku daimyō must be a Battle Maiden of exceptional character, and so it generally burdens the leader of the Battle Maidens. But while there have been rare times when the Utaku daimyō has not been a Battle Maiden, the station has only ever been fulfilled by a woman. Surprisingly, for a clan of idealists and individualists, this tradition largely goes unchallenged. Those whose gender does not align with womanhood traditionally defer higher stations as a matter of principle. Some may hold minor leadership positions in the absence of a qualified woman, but they are usually referred to by an alternate title (such as "high advisor"), and the position is considered temporary. There does exist a fraternal organization within the Utaku family: the Utaku Flying Army. These soldiers ride into battle but then dismount to fight, so as not to violate the law forbidding men to fight from horseback. They may not be as celebrated, but the Utaku infantry form the backbone of many campaigns, allowing cavalry to disengage after a charge.

#### Utaku Horse Culture

While nomadic traditions shaped the Unicorn Clan, the Utaku's entire lifestyle, including their customs and social mores, centers around the equestrian arts. For example, according to family custom, a promise made from horseback cannot be broken on pain of death or disgrace. This is why the family insists on signing treaties from horseback, and even marriages are conducted while mounted. While Utaku was said to be fond of animals, it was Utaku Shiko who began the family's equine traditions. According to legend, she raised her steeds from foals, and wild ponies willingly allowed her onto their backs. Historical records report that she even bestowed two "exemplary and brave" ponies with the rank of gunsō. Near the end of her life, Shiko completed a treatise on the philosophical unity between horse and rider, a concept known as Jinba-ittai. This became the foundation for the Utaku family's unique approach to horsemanship and the Battle Maidens' carefully guarded secrets for taming their legendary mounts.

> **A SACRED COMPANION**
>
> The Utaku reject the practice of "putting down" an injured horse; instead, they do everything they can to preserve the horse's quality of life. This is because Utaku traditions teach that a horse is not a mere beast of burden but a sacred animal, equal to humans. A common practice among the Utaku is that if a new horse absolutely refuses to cooperate or serve, rather than break the horse's spirit, they release it to the wild. To force a steed into service may draw the ire of the Blessed Herd or anger the Fortune of Horses.

> **THE UNICORN GUIDE TO CARING FOR HORSES**
>
> Many cultures the Unicorn encountered on their travels practiced shoeing their horses, which means nailing iron horseshoes to the bottom of hooves to protect a horse from injury. They also practiced gelding, which is castrating male horses to make them more obedient. Utaku Shiko regarded both these practices as barbaric and cruel, and Shinjo banned them outright except in specific circumstances. To this day, the Unicorn do not shoe their horses. Most of the clan's breeds have especially resilient hooves, and iron horseshoes are not necessary for the ways most samurai use their mounts. When needed, the Unicorn instead rely on woven straw sandals known as umazouri, made specially for horses. Likewise, gelding is reserved only for trade horses, so that other clans cannot breed their equals. Gelding warhorses is forbidden unless they are of a breed specifically for trade.

Utaku children traditionally begin supervised riding lessons within a year of learning to walk, and serious riding lessons start at age seven. This is when a child is entrusted with the care of a few foals. They are taught how to interpret a horse's behavior and earn its trust. By the time a child enters the dōjō, they are an advanced equestrian caregiver. All the while, Utaku children are closely observed, and those who show promise may be suggested for Horse Lord Disciple or Battle Maiden training. Regardless of their likely future path, only Utaku who can prove mastery of horsemanship are permitted to undertake the coming-of-age ceremony.

> **THE HEART MUST BE FREE**
>
> Due to cultural differences, many Utaku become angered or horrified at how other clans treat their horses. Upon discovering a stable where they feel horses are being mistreated, it is common for idealistic Utaku students to sneak there under cover of darkness and free all the horses within. Utaku sensei must endure the inevitable complaints of angry lords who discover the prank, yet few punishments ever seem to come from these incidents.

> **THE RESTRICTION OF BLESSED STEEDS**
>
> While it is forbidden for non-Battle Maidens to ride the blessed steeds, there is a notable legal exception: the Emperor. Even so, the Child of Heaven has rarely if ever attempted to mount one, perhaps out of respect for the Utaku's traditions…or because being thrown from a blessed steed's back would be poor for the Imperial reputation.

#### The Battle Maidens

The ways of the Battle Maidens are arguably the most famous of the Unicorn's martial traditions. Fighting mounted in full armor with naginata, horsebows, and dao, the Battle Maidens are regarded as the finest and most celebrated heavy cavalry units in the Empire. Considered a spiritual calling, the Battle Maiden's purpose is to follow in Utaku Shiko's footsteps through worship, preserving her ways, and making the world safer in her name. A Battle Maiden believes that Utaku Shiko's spirit guides and protects her, and she regards Bushidō tenets as religious virtues. Her rewards in the afterlife will depend on how worthy of Shiko's legacy she has proven to be. The Battle Maiden is a living vessel for Utaku Shiko herself, conviction and purity of spirit her greatest strengths.

The Battle Maidens have extremely high standards. Only women who carry the Utaku family name are permitted to train in the school, and even then, prospective students must meet incredibly stringent standards. Martial prowess and horsemanship are only a fraction of the required skills, as they must also demonstrate exceptional character and devotion. Those accepted into this prestigious sorority eventually learn the family's oldest secrets, including how to tame and ride the Utaku steeds.

A Battle Maiden's steed (see Utaku Steed in the Legend of the Five Rings roleplaying game core rulebook, page 327) does not come from the Utaku breeders, nor is it raised by human hands. Such warhorses are drafted from the Blessed Herd, a free-ranging herd of wild horses that roam the Utaku provinces under the Battle Maiden's vigil. According to an ancient law

whose breach is punishable by banishment from the clan, only a Battle Maiden may ride a blessed steed. Even without this law, the blessed Utaku steeds normally only accept worthy Battle Maidens on their backs, although history does recall a few exceptions.

Tradition dictates that the horse must choose its rider, or else it will never trust them. To this end, the Battle Maiden coming-of-age ceremony takes place near Utaku Seidō, and only when the free-roaming Blessed Herd is present. A hopeful recruit ventures among the herd with riding blanket and bridle and waits for a horse to approach. If one allows her onto its back, then both are welcomed among the Battle Maidens.

From this day on, both rider and steed are doshi, which means "dōjō partner." They train together and learn from one another. They both sleep beneath the stars, and they take meals together. They learn each other's body language and may even seem to communicate. Eventually the Battle Maiden does not even need a bridle to direct the horse, as both work wordlessly in tandem. This bond may seem supernatural to some, but in truth, there is nothing supernatural about it. Through time, trial, and mutual respect, both have come to understand the other in a way impossible to put to words.

### The Lands of the Utaku

Throughout the central and eastern plains of the Unicorn, the Utaku family's provinces are vast and peaceful but contain little of note. Farms, villages, and roads are sparse throughout the grassy countryside, leaving plenty of flat land for horses to wander and riders to thunder over as they cross the ancestral plains. Only the westernmost lands feature foothills and fertile valleys, with rivers rolling down into the southern lands of the Ide.

#### Battle Maiden Castle

A splendid palatial estate, the Utaku's ancestral keep stands less than a day's ride from Far Traveler Castle. Overlooking Dragon's Heart Forest to the west, the keep is otherwise surrounded by flat plains, allowing "visitors" to be spotted from miles away. Because it was built by the remnants Shinjo left behind, it is technically one of the oldest castles in Rokugan, although three-quarters of the castle was added after the Utaku's return.

Yet more impressive than the castle's blend of foreign and ancient architecture, its bell-shaped domes, or its constructed cascades is its greatest treasure: the

> **EXCERPTS FROM THE WRITINGS OF UTAKU SHIKO**
>
> - In a herd, the first horse to move is the lowest in rank. By urging a horse to follow, you are only proving it is better than you are. This is also true of warriors.
> - A horse bows to all horses above them in the herd. So to tame a horse, one must prove oneself to be highest. The first step? Join their herd.
> - When two horses meet, what do they do? They snort at one another. When a horse approaches, snort into its nostrils. You are saying "hello." Be sure to laugh at yourself afterward.
> - Horses regard us as predators. And why wouldn't they? Humans are the greatest killers to ever walk these lands. To earn a horse's trust, you must prove you are different.

Utaku ancestral stables. Often mistaken for a second keep, these stables house the breeding and training grounds of many different steeds. As the estate's most important feature, the stables enjoy dedicated protective walls and remain under watchful guard at all times.

It is uncommon for visitors to be hosted here, as there is little business for the other clans here, and the Utaku are not inclined to invite guests. For outsiders to spend even a few hours within the concentric walls is a rare honor, even if offered only out of necessity.

Easily overlooked on the hilly border of Isei province is a tiny shrine far from any road or village. While locals call it Utaku's Shrine, it is known abroad by its poetic name: the Shrine of Shimmering Grasses. This name comes from the golden hakone grass carpeting the landscape, which appears to sparkle in direct sunlight. Those passing beneath the torii arch into the fenceless shrine grounds often report an overwhelming sense of tranquility and peace.

Because of the stillness, travelers sometimes mistake the shrine as abandoned. There are no bells, no songs, and no calls to worship. Many of the shrine's keepers swear vows of silence in honor of Utaku's own quiet nature.

> **THE UTAKU STEEDS**
>
> The typical Unicorn warhorse is a product of the Utaku Horse Lord Disciples, but a Battle Maiden's steed is drafted from the wild horses of the Blessed Herd. Legends suggest these horses are descended from the children of Shinjo herself, while others say they are connected to the blessed ki-rin. Stronger, smarter, and larger than mere riding ponies, they are said to reject all but the most devout women riders.

> **UTAKU SEIDŌ**
>
> As visitors leave Utaku Seidō, one of the keepers may approach with an offer to paint a Fortune's name on their right arm. Many in the Unicorn believe that doing this attracts the Fortune and grants their blessing.

> **THE FIVE WINDS CONCLAVE**
>
> In ancient times, the Great Khans of the Ujik were elected in a great feast called the quriltai. The Moto Ujik broke this tradition when they pledged themselves to Shinjo and named her the Khan of Khans, but the Unicorn adapted it in their own way.
> Every year, in autumn during the Month of the Dog, the Unicorn Clan Champion holds the Five Winds Conclave on the Utaku Plains. Over the course of six days, samurai of the Unicorn gather from across the Empire and set their yurts on the plains. The gathering is open to any Unicorn samurai, regardless of station or rank—even daimyō and lowly buke are said to brush shoulders during the conclave.
> The gathered samurai congregate and engage in competitions and sponsored activities. On the seventh and final day, the Iuchi daimyō ceremoniously invites the spirit of the ki-rin to join, and a great feast is held in honor of the clan founders. This is an event intended to let Unicorn samurai reconnect and remember their shared heritage, as it was always Shinjo's hope that all of her clan's people would see each other as family.

### RUMORS OF UTAKU'S SHRINE

> **RUMORS OF UTAKU'S SHRINE**
>
> "I heard that Utaku's daikyū (longbow) enshrined in the honden is a fake! The real daikyū vanished from the shrine a month ago, and no one is certain who took it."
> "Do you see how the horses eat the flowering moss at the foot of the Mother-Sister statues? That is why they are more intelligent than typical horses—those blooms are blessed by the Fortune of Horses!"
> "If you hide in the grass on the seventh day of the Month of the Horse during a certain night hour, a ki-rin will appear and reveal the one you are destined to marry."

### SACRED STABLES

> **SACRED STABLES**
>
> In ancient times, lords would dedicate ponies to the local shrine as gifts for the enshrined Fortune or ancestor. Such a pony would be regarded as the spirit's steed, known as a shinme, or "sacred horse." When the Unicorn Clan returned from exploring the outside world, most Rokugani had abandoned this practice or were gifting statues instead of real ponies. Ironically, now the last observers of this tradition, the Unicorn build most of their shrines with a dedicated stable that typically houses one or more shinme.

Adding to this is the shrine's apparent "disrepair." The fields and bamboo thicket nearby are wild and overgrown, and an ancient horse-chestnut tree grows out of the roof of the ivy-coated honden. The truth is that Utaku herself planted the horse-chestnut tree before venturing into the Shadowlands with the Seven Thunders, and so the shrine was carefully built around it. Chestnuts gathered from this tree are used to make a special pastry that is eaten during festivals.

Another feature is the "horseflower garden," filled with herbs and blooms that horses especially enjoy. These include purple valerian and verdant fenugreek from beyond Rokugan's borders, alongside native strains of marigold and catmint. Watching over the gardens are the Mother-Sister statues, depicting Utaku and her daughter. Battle Maidens make frequent pilgrimages here, laying offerings at Utaku's feet for courage and at Shiko's feet for devotion.

A samurai seeking counsel with a Battle Maiden is wise to look here, as Battle Maidens are always coming and going, meditating within the shrine or perusing its collection of accounts from Utaku Shiko's life. Aspiring Battle Maidens spend the night prior to their coming-of-age ceremony here, and designated visitor quarters are maintained year-round for these purposes.

#### Fields of the Blessed Herd

While most (if not all) of the Unicorn's shrines feature a stable housing the sacred pony, this feature is notably absent from the Shrine of Shimmering Grasses. This is because the shrine is a favorite grazing spot for the Blessed Herd, which roams freely throughout the nearby Utaku meadows. Coming and going as they please, the steeds seem to favor the tender grasses growing near the Mother-Daughter statues, even grazing on the moon-touched blooms of the flowering moss clinging to the carved stone. While visitors are advised to keep a respectful distance, sometimes a blessed horse will approach, a sign of good fortune.

- Affinity with Horses: Social; Interpersonal
- Local Folklorist: Scholar; Mental
- Overprotective: Martial; Mental

### Path to Inner Peace (Invocation)

As a Support action, may make a TN 2 Theology (Water) check targeting a character at range 0–2. If Asagao succeeds, the target removes 3 fatigue, plus 1 per bonus success. A target can be affected by Path to Inner Peace only once per scene.

## IUCHI

With only a novice's understanding of kami invocation and a handful of rituals, the polymath Iuchi could hardly have been called a "great shugenja." Yet this very circumstance was in his favor, for his lack of preconceived notions enabled him to understand the sorcerous traditions of other cultures. Whenever he encountered a new magic he eagerly studied it, absorbing all that he could through the lens of Shinsei's Tao. Because invoking the kami proved increasingly ineffective the farther the Ki-Rin Clan traveled, the clan's people became increasingly dependent on Iuchi and his students. He knew that their very survival depended on how well they could adapt to the new spiritual landscapes. Shinjo needed an advisor who could protect her people from supernatural harm. Although the original techniques Iuchi learned from the Rempet focused on compelling and trapping powerful spirits by force, it was through a collective effort that Iuchi and his students found that coming to know a spirit's true name could be done through building a relationship and developing trust just as readily as bending a spirit to one's will.

The Iuchi still practice their founder's techniques to this day. As the lone masters of meishōdō ("the way of names"), they are the predominant spiritual advisors of the Unicorn, and their ancestral traditions make them priests of the kami. But they are also keepers of a terrible secret, one that could bring the clan low were it ever discovered.

### Reputation and Values

The Iuchi ancestral tradition is similar to that of other shugenja: as a religious order, the Iuchi are expected to eschew violence, advocate for spirits, and tend to the harmony of the lands. Additionally, the Iuchi are experts on spirits and sorceries from beyond the Empire. This knowledge may come in handy as word of Rokugan's prosperity and reputation spreads throughout the world.

The things the Iuchi value most are knowledge and open-mindedness. While their founder relied on Shinsei's Tao, he also believed that there is no one true interpretation of the world. The Iuchi still strive to synchronize as many traditions as they can, from both within and outside the Empire, into a singular theory. Their own unique art of meishōdō is as much based on the arcane practices of foreign sorcerers as it is on the spiritual arts of Rokugani shugenja.

Iuchi values place a heavy emphasis on personal ambition. The drive to discover and to accumulate power is considered virtuous. Ambition can be the drive to become a better person, to protect a fragile community, or to make the world safer for all people everywhere. After all, much good in the Empire has been accomplished due to the earnest efforts of ambitious people.

Similarly, the Iuchi believe that no source of power is inherently "bad." Power sources are ultimately tools that must be deployed with wisdom; evil comes from human acts, not from the tools they wield. Still, the Iuchi also believe that evil can be caused indirectly; when something goes awry, it is the custom of the Iuchi to accept all consequences. They place great weight on personal responsibility: if evil results due to ignorance, their obligation is to document it, correct what is possible, and endure the rest themselves.

> **GREEN HORDE**
>
> When the Five Winds, the five families, of the Ki-Rin parted, Iuchi led the Green Horde south in search of hidden secrets. They were the only horde never to return; there is no record of what became of them, and no one knows Iuchi's fate.

This means that an Iuchi must be objective and evenhanded. An Iuchi realizes how seductive true power can be. They need only look to the Unicorn's time among the Rempet and the fate of the sorcerer-king Nephrentep—a necromancer tyrant who was overthrown by the Ki-Rin and al-Qamari—as proof of how easily one can stray from the path of virtue. Iuchi teachings advocate regular self-reflection and the embrace of Shinseist virtues to prevent an individual from going too far.

Other clans are often suspicious of the Iuchi, regarding them as untrustworthy sorcerers. However, while they may not invoke the spirits conventionally, Iuchi Meishōdō Masters are still priests of the kami, and their drive to understand the elements is ultimately still religious. Those who eventually understand this usually change their view of the Iuchi. But to the rest of the Empire, the Iuchi are keepers of dangerous secrets and terrible powers. That suits the Iuchi just fine.

### Culture and Traditions

Because the Iuchi have proven invaluable to the Unicorn for centuries, they enjoy significant autonomy. The other families occasionally approach the Iuchi with requests, but for the most part, Iuchi are left to their own devices. The Iuchi practice more experimentation than any other shugenja family in the Empire. They know well that there is no "one path" to any destination. They are always eager to learn other practices, as this often leads to insights they can use in their own meditations.

However, when it comes to teaching their own arts, the Iuchi are quite secretive. They know that most would object to the origins of their work, and without proper context, many would regard what they do as blasphemy. This results in an insular, self-driven culture, one where self-discovery is as celebrated as other forms of exploration. The Iuchi constantly test their own limits, if only to learn what they are capable of.

Many Iuchi find a calling to serve their clan. Among these are the Disciples of the Horse Lord. Employing methods derived from the research of Iuchi Meiko, one of Iuchi's children who accompanied the Purple Horde of Utaku Shiko, the Horse Lords are specialized Meishōdō Masters. Using horse-shaped meishōdō talismans, they can invoke the Fortune of Horses. By adorning horses

> **THE DOOMHUNTER**
>
> The Ki-Rin Clan attracted the attention of all manner of supernatural beings throughout its travels, especially in the Empire of Rempet. While the spirits of the Emerald Empire lived harmoniously alongside mortals, the spirits of other lands more often seemed at odds with their people. Iuchi noted the prevalence of wards, banes, and "spells" dedicated to compelling and banishing spirits in these lands.
> When one of Iuchi's students studied the ways of al-Qamari, an organization that opposed the life-extending Sun Kings, it began the Iuchi family's most unusual and esoteric tradition: that of the Doomhunter. Incorporating ancient Rempet teachings, the Doomhunter was instrumental in protecting the Ki-Rin from djinn and powerful undead.
> The Doomhunter is a Meishōdō Master whose purpose is to hunt and kill immortals. Wielding the power of their target's name and deploying all manner of folk traditions, the Doomhunter seeks undead and other lifeless beings that threaten the clan. It is also their job to watch their fellow Iuchi for signs of obsession and iniquity. If an Iuchi's studies go too far, or bend into blood magic, it is the Doomhunter's obligation to stop them before they become a threat.
> Additionally, the Doomhunter teachings contain many secrets for breaking curses; the Doomhunter is often sought out by those who suffer such misfortunes. While a Doomhunter can undo or reduce the effects of most curses, some might say "the cure is worse than the affliction," as these cures almost always involve some difficult and risky task. Exceptional curses, such as the generational affliction of the Scorpion Clan's Yogo family, have proven too powerful for even a Doomhunter to break.
> Breaking curses is the additional function of a Doomhunter that gives the tradition its name: in addressing curses, the Doomhunter draws the ire of terrible beings—and their curses and hexes—upon themselves and away from their clan. This makes their position an exceptionally dangerous one, and it necessitates that there only ever be one Doomhunter at any time. When one falls, another ultimately rises to claim the mantle. As they accumulate jinxes throughout their lives, the Doomhunter further distances themself from their clan. As a result, while theirs is an admired position, it may ultimately be a lonely one.

with these trinkets, they have the ability to empower a samurai's steed, to speak with horses directly, or even to summon an ethereal mount. The Iuchi Horse Lord Disciples are also foremost experts in equine medicine, and they have saved the lives of many injured steeds.

Meishōdō is the most significant of the Iuchi family's practices, and it is the legacy of Iuchi himself. A typical Iuchi carries several meishōdō talismans, each with a specific purpose. The family's entire culture revolves around the art of crafting and employing these talismans. Their most significant religious and artistic contribution to the clan, this art is unlike any other spiritual art in the Empire. This is with good reason: it is derived from foreign sorceries, the same rituals the Rempet pharaohs used to enslave the djinn, reinterpreted into an art that can be somewhat more amenable to the spirits themselves. Very few people know the true nature of these charms; to most, they are simple jewelry, albeit with special properties.

In truth, the original practice of meishōdō was wholly the art of binding spirits and then compelling them to manifest. The Iuchi found the practice concerning, however, and over time discovered that spirits are often willing to share their true name to those they trust. The Iuchi has become adept at earning and keeping the trust of spirits, who willingly shelter inside provided talismans and manifest when asked.

A Meishōdō Master is able to create a special talisman that acts as a vessel for a unique spirit, though the methods employed to create such objects are deeply protected secrets among the family. The talismans are inscribed with runes that describe the intended elemental manifestation along with the spirit's "true name." A name can be ascertained through careful research and observation, though the simplest way is to interact with the spirit and gain its confidence. In the old way of meishōdō, spirits are made to give up their true names through force or deception. The runes required to hold the spirit are more complicated even with the true name, however, because the enslaved being is constantly fighting to free themselves.

> **IUCHI RUMORS**
>
> "One of the governor's children released a djinni from a bottle, and it escaped into the countryside. But do you think anyone is doing anything about it? Of course not—that would mean he would have to punish his only daughter!"
> "They say Kyoji was severed from the family for practicing blood magic, but even to the end he insisted that he was merely following the teachings of Iuchi. What a twisted mind he must have possessed to believe such a thing!"
> "The Emperor has commanded that we teach the Hidden Guard how to practice meishōdō. While I do not doubt his wisdom, what will it mean for our family's ways when they can be possessed by others?"

Though a true name is powerful and linked to the very core of a being, with enough time, the instructional runes that describe the manifestation can be damaged. Damage to a talisman risks partly freeing the spirit, allowing it to wreak havoc when it manifests.

The talisman then becomes a focus for the spirit. This grants the user significant power over the bound entity. The Meishōdō Master can use the talisman to call upon the spirit to manifest, or to instead invoke the inscribed effect, removing the normal prerequisite offerings needed. A spirit that dwells within a talisman cannot leave unless the Meishōdō Master wills it, or the talisman becomes damaged enough that the spirit is no longer fully bound.

The Iuchi have moved away from the traditional form of meishōdō largely because the practice differed so greatly from other spiritual arts of the Empire. Traditional meishōdō foregoes the exchange of offerings, compelling the kami rather than asking for aid. It changes the power dynamic, making the shugenja the master, the spirit the servant. Comparisons may be drawn between the Asahina and Agasha's creation of talismans through their tsangusuri art, and many other shugenja use charms and talismans as tiny portable shrines for spirits. But the kami are not bound to these items in the same way, nor are these kami compelled to do a mortal's bidding. Meishōdō talismans use a spirit's power without their permission. Without care, the talismans can become prisons.

Even though the current practice of meishōdō in the Empire relies much more heavily on the building of trust and relationships with spirits, those outside of the Unicorn Clan often misunderstand the practice, conflate it with the older practices, and consider the use of such teachings to be disrespectful at best and heretical at worst. Some would like to see meishōdō banned on the same heretical grounds as blood magic.

When the Iuchi returned to Rokugan, they found that the kami did not answer their invocations. Neither did the Fortunes, nor the blessed ancestors of the Ki-Rin. Whether the spirits no longer recognized them as belonging to the Emerald Empire, or some deal or ritual had somehow cut them off from their homeland's spirits, or Iuchi himself had never truly had the shugenja's gift to start with, the cold fact remained: the Iuchi could not commune with the spirits.

But their meishōdō techniques proved to work just fine. Through use of a talismantic offering, they could request an audience with an elemental kami or convince a Fortune to answer.

Not even the other Unicorn families are aware of the secret of the Iuchi family. Willingly revealing it to anyone outside the family would trigger a curse that is inscribed on each Iuchi by the Meishōdō Masters traditionally overseeing their birth. If it were ever discovered that the Iuchi are unable to invoke the kami as shugenja, the implications could prompt the removal of the family from the religious institutions of the Empire.

The Iuchi do not see what they do as heretical at all. They still treat the spirits with respect (at least from their perspective), and their teachings do not depart from the Tao. Where spirits are malicious, their arts have benefited humankind. Where the elements are out of balance, their talismans can restore harmony.

Occupying the large expanse of southeastern territories, the Iuchi provinces are the loneliest of the Unicorn's. These vast stretches of uninterrupted grasslands are home to herds of wild ponies, deer, and many other forms of wildlife, all thriving in the absence of villages and towns. In contrast to the flats, territory along the western border of Iuchi lands is mountainous and rocky, similar to the steppes of northern Rokugan. These mountains are home to several diamond veins; as a result, mining communities form some of the only villages of the Iuchi. To the east, Three Sides River divides the Unicorn provinces from the lands of the Lion Clan, and the southernmost portion of the Seikitsu Pass marks the only passage through the mountains from both Scorpion provinces and the clans to the south.

The ancestral home of the Iuchi is one of the best-fortified keeps in Unicorn lands, not because of an army presence, but because it is built into the foothills of the nearby Seikitsu Mountains.

Its poetic name, Gatherer of Winds Castle, comes from the violent winds that sometimes sweep across the plains below. It is one of the oldest castles in Rokugan, built for Lady Shinjo at the dawn of the Empire. Had the Ki-Rin never departed the Empire, it might even have become the ancestral seat of the Shinjo family.

In the following centuries, the Lion Clan having occupied Ki-Rin lands, the Akodo family attempted to use the castle as a launching ground for military campaigns. According to legend, the Lion inhabitants would consistently wake up in the fields below the castle with no knowledge of how they had gotten there. Victims claimed to have dreamt of riding a mysterious deer or pony, one with a flaming mane and twisted antlers, only to awaken far from their bed. So it went, night after night, until at last the castle seneschals

awoke in the nearby Dreamer's Forest. After that, the Lion abandoned the keep, and it remained so until the Unicorn's return.

Restored by the Iuchi family, the keep is among the most splendid in Rokugan, with a welcoming interior filled with simple comforts and a vast library. A collection of fine art, both Rokugani and foreign, decorates its many halls. Guests may be taken aback by the Iuchi's hospitality, and they may find their stay very pleasant, so long as they do not venture into the Gatherer of Winds Temple, which is off-limits to all but the family elite.

Shiro Iuchi's castle town contains the most concentrated population in the entirety of Iuchi lands. With access to the mountain range's steam vents, the town's natural onsen bath houses are among the greatest in the Empire. A popular tourist destination, the town has begun to attract some seedy elements, and while the magistrates struggle to maintain its integrity, the Iuchi lords themselves do not seem to care.

### Grandfather Iuchi's Tower

Grandfather Iuchi's Tower is the largest and best-stocked depository of foreign texts. It contains an impressive selection of books, scrolls, and artifacts collected by the Ki-Rin in their travels. It also displays several maps drawn by those explorers and a wooden globe that has been constructed based on the knowledge gained during their journeys. It is tended to by a man known as Grandfather, a very old Iuchi Meishōdō Master who has trained some of the most promising students in the art of the meishōdō craft.

Although it has taken generations of covert work and is not yet complete, the library also boasts a catalog of nearly every shrine in the Empire—and for many of these shrines, the true name of the enshrined kami. The intention is that if the Iuchi are ever needed in an area with which they are unfamiliar, they will have at least the enshrined kami they can make a meishōdō talisman for. The Iuchi do not reveal this catalog to outsiders, as some clans may object to its existence.

### The Dreamer's Forest

South of Shiro Iuchi is a lone patch of woods in the midst of the rolling plains, surrounded by still pairs of back-to-back komainu, or lion-dog, statues. Those who approach may hear the statues growling, a warning to turn back. This cursed forest is off-limits, filled with malicious kansen, corrupted spirits, desperate to escape. Indeed, this forest would have slowly spread to engulf the entire landscape had the Iuchi Meishōdō Masters not contained it. Every night, priests walk the deep

trench at the forest's perimeter, purifying the ground, replacing any spent wards and talismans, and above all, ignoring the seductive calls of the woods beyond. It is believed that Yume-dō, the Realm of Dreams, holds this forest in its grasp. Those who venture too deeply into the Dreamer's Forest ultimately fall into a deep sleep, their bodies gradually hardening into bark, their feet taking root in the cursed soil. Some whisper that the wood of such trees is a potent offering for kansen and is the reason why so many of these Shadowlands spirits now dwell here. Mahō-tsukai—blood magic practitioners—sometimes risk wandering the woods to gather such "blood kindling" or to conduct profane rituals. For this reason, entering the forest without the Iuchi daimyō's blessing is punishable by death.

#### Iuchi Itsuki

One of a handful of Meishōdō Masters entrusted with this task, Iuchi Itsuki has the duty of maintaining the wards and statues that contain the Dreamer's Forest. Twice every season, guarded with protective talismans and accompanied by trusted companions, he patrols the outermost portions of the woods, checking to be sure the malicious forest is still bound. While Itsuki would never outwardly complain, to his trusted friends he has long bemoaned his station, for he has always felt that he was meant for something greater. During his most recent venture, one of the talismans spoke to him, claiming to be an innocent trapped fox spirit, not a malicious baku—a dream spirit—of Yume-dō. She pleaded to be released, promising to share some power if only Itsuki would free her from the prison of the charm. Itsuki knows better than to trust this voice in his head, and yet he cannot be fully sure that the spirit is lying. If she is telling the truth, then he would be complicit in an innocent spirit's unjust imprisonment. And ever since, he has seen the spirit in his dreams: a shackled fox maiden, begging to be freed. With each passing night of interrupted sleep, he feels his resolve slipping a little more.

#### The Ide Family Mon

### IDE

It is said that when Ide initially joined Shinjo's journey, many wondered what use he would be. He was no warrior, and he could barely remain on his steed. But Shinjo saw great potential in the clever, likable man, and since Utaku Shiko seemed taken with him, Shinjo did not object. It was not long before Ide made his usefulness clear, conducting trade, smoothing over tensions both within and without, and keeping accurate histories of Shinjo's travels. But it was in the Empire of Rempet, when Shinjo fell into the hands of the Sun Kings and was held hostage for a profane ritual, that Ide rose into legend. It was he who found allies in al-Qamari and orchestrated Shinjo's rescue. Rewarded with his own family and entrusted with the leadership of an entire horde, Ide went from a simple merchant to a clan daimyō.

The Ide still remain the clan's primary representatives. As administrators, they oversee the day-to-day operations that keep the clan prosperous and politically shielded. They take on the Unicorn's social burdens so that the other families can remain independent and free. Chroniclers, diplomats, and exceptional traders, the humble and peaceful Ide are the ones to whom the Unicorn Clan owes its comfortable position.

> **GOLDEN HORDE**
>
> When the Five Winds of the Ki-Rin parted, Ide remained in the valley with the Golden Horde. Befriending the peoples nearby, the Golden Horde founded the City of the Ki-Rin and waited for the other hordes to return.

### Reputation and Values

The Ide are the least militant of the Unicorn families, as Ide himself was a peaceful and very patient man. They strive to keep things harmonious while protecting the clan's interests, relying on trade, diplomacy, and simple friendliness to meet the clan's goals. They see things in the long term, being more than willing to endure small inconveniences if it means furthering the clan's position. Yet what the Ide are willing to compromise, the other families may not be, a fact they sometimes forget.

Other clan courtiers might offer the Ide their sympathies, for representing a clan like the Unicorn—willful, wild, resistant to compromise—is not an easy task. The Ide family formed around the need for administration and cultural exchange, and their traditions evolved to suit those needs. Most become merchants, courtiers, messengers, and arbiters. Many times, the Ide find themselves thrust into conflict resolution between the passionate Unicorn families, and each time, they do their best to keep the peace.

The values and philosophy of the Ide can be summarized by their own founder, who once said, "Violence is the language of defeat, swords the tongues of the lost." The way of the family is summarized as "open-hand diplomacy," which likens the ideal diplomat to one whose hand is always extended, in friendship or in offering, but always empty of threats. To this end, most Ide are staunch pacifists. It is against the family's ways to carry weapons, even in self-defense or in potentially dangerous situations. Ide himself was never armed, relying instead on his keen mind.

Few clan families wish to be seen as "meek," but the Ide do. It means they are underestimated, and that is a powerful position for one who can leverage it. The family has very few enemies due to their amiable nature, and most consider them fair, reasonable, and accommodating. They are especially beloved by the peasantry due to their great number of charitable works.

After the Unicorn's return, the Ide's negotiation prowess soon caught the eye of the Imperial families. The Ide have become a natural well of talent for the Imperial peacekeepers, a special organization of Imperial ambassadors. Recruited from many clans by the Imperial Herald, the peacekeepers' purpose is to prevent or limit interclan conflicts, stopping them before they can escalate into needless bloodshed. The Ide have a rich history doing just that.

### Culture and Traditions

While some Ide train in other family dōjō to become magistrates or bushi, Ide culture naturally leans toward mercantile and courtly pursuits. This is because the way of the family evolved to fulfill those needs. The family claims holdings in markets throughout the Empire, and while other clan families find money handling distasteful, the Ide have no such qualms. Ide traders are resourceful, and with several commercial ventures even along the Silk Road, the family can generally obtain whatever it is they need.

The Ide also generally handle the wealth of the Unicorn. For instance, Utaku breeders may sell gelded ponies to the other clans, but the actual deals might be managed by an Ide trader. The Ide also shoulder logistics demands in times of war, keeping Unicorn forces well stocked and supplies flowing. A single warhorse requires roughly ten times the food and supplies of an ashigaru soldier, so even as noncombatants, the Ide are kept quite busy.

As venerators of Daikoku, the Fortune of Wealth, individual Ide are expected to devote a portion of their time and wealth to the benefit of the less fortunate in any way they deem fit. As such, the Ide traditionally practice great charity, donating excess food and supplies to poor villages, hiring craftspeople to make improvements, or even taking on the tax burden of underproducing towns. Ide magistrates sometimes translate complex law documents so that they are easier for laypeople to understand. They may even arrange for the sale of goods at marketplaces without accepting any portion of the proceeds.

The way Ide teach their children is somewhat different from what is typical, in that most of a child's upbringing is spent on the estates of other samurai families. Arranged by the dōjō, each Ide child spends at least half of their schooling in other lands, and adolescents spend at least two years in the provinces of another clan or, in some cases, the lands of the Caliphate. The hope is that having to adapt to another family's ways will shape them into better problem solvers and instill respect for other cultures. And if they happen to forge lifelong bonds while they are there, then all the better!

### The Lands of the Ide

Entrusted with the southern border, the Ide maintain the most densely populated provinces of any Unicorn family. Encompassing more farms, villages, and towns, Ide lands more closely resemble the rest of the Empire. Their border, the largest unguarded border of the Unicorn, is maintained not with arms or outrider patrols, but through diplomacy.

Filled with rolling hills and farmlands, the Ide provinces are dominated by Chrysanthemum Petal Lake, one of the largest in Rokugan. The River of the Unexpected Hero bisects their provinces and eventually winds into the Shinomen Forest. The Ide's closest

neighbors to the east are the remote estates of the Miya Imperial family, along with the Soshi family of the Scorpion. To the south, the forbidding Shinomen Forest creates a natural barrier against invasion, and the lands of the Hare Clan are but a stone's throw away.

Dotted throughout Ide lands, especially away from the lakes and floodplains, are raised hills covered in unmortared stone. Built by Lion Clan ancestors, these burial mounds contain subterranean tombs that predate the Imperial declaration requiring the cremation of all dead. When the Unicorn reclaimed these lands, the Lion were forced to withdraw, leaving behind the entombed remains and the relics laid beside them. Many Lion still resent the Ide for claiming these ancestral burial mounds, but thanks to Imperial edict, there is little the Lion can do.

Rising above the northern banks of Chrysanthemum Petal Lake and the surrounding lakeside town, Kyūden Ide is the only "traditional" keep in Unicorn lands. Designed for comfort, displaying the clan's wealth while offering virtually no defense against invading forces, Great Day Castle is nonetheless considered one of the safest places in the Empire. It is the only Unicorn castle able to host an Imperial Winter Court, having been built for precisely that purpose.

After the Unicorn were finally recognized as a Great Clan, the Ide realized they needed a place to host the Imperial retinue, should the Child of Heaven ever wish to visit the Unicorn. The Ide poured out their coffers and owed many favors, but the main abode was finished in five years, and within nine, the palace was completed. However, during the final year of construction, the Scorpion Clan questioned the ability of the palace to accommodate 250 guests, a requirement for the kyūden, or palace, distinction. This and the political efforts of both the Scorpion and Lion effectively stymied the Imperial decree that would have granted the palace this elevated status. For several decades, Great Day Castle was known as Shiro Ide, ineligible to host a Winter Court.

Eventually, during an especially hot and rainy summer, an unexpected mudslide waylaid and stranded the Imperial cavalcade on its way to Kyūden Miya. A Unicorn scouting party soon discovered and extricated them, and the Ide daimyō (who was conveniently among the outriders) offered Great Day Castle as temporary accommodations. The Emperor's family greatly enjoyed their stay, and the Emperor's wife was especially taken each morning by the blooming chrysanthemums across the crystalline lake. By the time a Bayushi procession arrived to retrieve them, the Emperor was so impressed that he had bestowed Great Day Castle—now Great Day Palace—with the kyūden title. The keep hosted an Imperial Court that very winter.

This is a story the Ide enjoy telling, and any who remark on the convenience of a sudden mudslide waylaying the Imperial procession receive only a knowing smile.

While the Ide are not exactly nomadic, they are no strangers to the road, often hosting diplomatic functions away from their homelands. To this end, they maintain a fleet of mobile palatial tents known as the Golden Yurts. Originally conceived as temporary living quarters during Great Day Castle's construction, these massive luxury yurts are made from the finest materials and incorporate a kyūden's comforts. In essence, they are a portable palace that the Ide can use to bring the comforts of the courts with them.

While usually found along Chrysanthemum Petal Lake, the Golden Yurts are also deployed in more remote surroundings, especially where rustic scenery—and the accompanying privacy—suits the Ide's needs. Many find the novelty of the Golden Yurts intriguing, and it is becoming more common for visiting dignitaries to subtly request a short stay in one of these luxury tents, especially in the summer when the lake is in full bloom.

> **THE UNEXPECTED HERO**
>
> The River of the Unexpected Hero is named for an event that nearly cost untold lives had it not been for a hero's timely intervention. As the tale goes, a farmer who had snuck away from the Chrysanthemum Festival to go fishing in Chrysanthemum Petal Lake happened to spot goblins mulling about the lakefront. Even though he could get in trouble for unauthorized fishing in the lake, he nonetheless reported what he had seen to a patrol. It turned out that the goblins had escaped past the Kaiu Wall, intent on poisoning both the lake and the river. The farmer aided the patrol in holding off the creatures until proper warriors could arrive. For his bravery, the farmer—forever to be known as the unexpected hero—was elevated to samurai status and made an honored retainer of the Ide.

> **IDE RUMORS**
>
> "The Ide supposedly never carry weapons, but I swear that I heard swords clashing near the garden's cherry tree grove this morning. What is that old, abandoned warehouse there used for anyway?"
> "Grandmother said that the lake's chrysanthemums were planted by the seventeenth Emperor after he lost his son. A tea made from the white ones cures any malady, but I've never seen a white chrysanthemum growing here."

### Ide Yūto

As the son of high-ranking diplomats, wayward slacker Ide Yūto had few obligations and could seek whatever path he wished. So it was to his family's great distress when, while staying at Far Traveler Castle, Yūto vanished without a trace. Months passed, and his family was prepared to accept his unexplained death when he suddenly arrived at Great Day Castle, road-worn and grinning ear to ear.

He claimed that he had seen a glimmer under the waters of Water Dragon Lake, and that after diving as far as he could go, he had surfaced in the territory of the Centipede Clan on the eastern coast of Rokugan. He had spent the last two seasons traveling back across the Empire, sending several messages that ultimately never reached his family. While unbelievable, his story was supported by the various trinkets and gifts he had brought back, including silks from Moshi looms.

Yūto has craved adventure since that day. He is often seen accosting travelers as they pass through the town near Kyūden Ide, hoping for a chance to return to a life of excitement.

### Advantages / Disadvantages

- Tall Tales: Scholar; Interpersonal
- Unfettered: Social, Trade; Mental
- Impetuous Risk-Taker: Martial; Mental
Gear (equipped): Traveling clothes (Physical 2, Durable, Mundane, Subtle), traveling supplies, various trinkets, string of koku

Companion: Riding pony ("Kushami") (see Horse [Rokugani Pony] in the core rulebook, page 326)

### Quick Assessment

As a Support action, Yūto may target an NPC within range 0–3 who is currently engaged in conversation or combat. He discerns the target's demeanor, vigilance, and composure (and may reveal them to his allies).

### Unassuming

When Yūto makes a check targeting a character with a higher status rank, he treats the target's vigilance as 1 lower.

## MOTO

It was in the brutal Plains of Wind and Stone that the fledgling Ki-Rin first encountered the Ujik nomads. Although Shinjo's clan was starving in the harsh climate, her people were numerous and well armed, appearing to the Ujik as prideful invaders marching across the deserts and steppes. The Ki-Rin became targets for the Ujik's raids, the mobile tribes atop their massive war steeds picking supplies from the hapless clan like buzzards from carrion.

That is, until Shinjo and Utaku Shiko managed to capture a handful of Ujik horses in a daring counter-raid, adopting tactics similar to those the Ujik had deployed and managing to fend off the party that followed them. Shinjo's clan braced for the inevitable retribution, but when the massive Ujik force appeared, it was under a flag of peace. The Ujik elders had watched Shinjo's people from afar and, finally impressed by their resourcefulness and daring, decided to make room for them. Shinjo met with a council of their leaders and walked away with the promise of peace, so long as her people would respect the Ujik ways.

With time, the Ki-Rin grew especially close with a specific Ujik tribe. The two learned from one another, both growing and thriving in the steppes. Within a century, the Ki-Rin were riding atop Ujik desert horses, and the two groups had traded and intermarried so much that their khan decided they should become as one. Trading swords with Shinjo in accordance with their custom, the khan pledged his people to her cause, becoming the daimyō of the Moto family, the saviors of the clan.

### Reputation and Values

Even after hundreds of years, the Unicorn have never forgotten what their clan owes to the Moto family. Shinjo's generational expedition would have ended just as it started had the Ujik tribes not extended their hand in friendship and shown them how to survive. Trade with the Ujik tribe that would become the Moto kept the Ki-Rin fed and prosperous. The clan's nomadic traditions are Ujik in origin, as are many of the Unicorn's most important innovations, like its unique type of saddle, its adoption of the stirrup, and its style of horse archery. Indeed, while horse archery fell out of practice in Rokugan, the Moto's mastery of the art proved invaluable in the lands beyond, and they shared this art with the entire clan. It is because of this that the Moto share a special kinship with the Shinjo and are afforded far more freedom than is typical for a Great Clan family.

> **WHITE HORDE**
>
> When the Five Winds of the Ki-Rin parted, Moto Chabi Khan led the White Horde north across the Burning Sands.

While the other Unicorn families assimilated into Rokugan as best they were able, settling in the Ki-Rin's ancestral lands, the Moto had no desire to do so. They were permitted to remain in the steppes just beyond the borders of Rokugan. Khanbulak, the great western gateway into the Empire, is the Moto's lone holding within Rokugan. When other clans ask about this arrangement, the Shinjo simply say that the Moto are tasked with guarding the western border. So long as they serve the Empire, what does it matter where they live?

It is perhaps to the Unicorn's benefit that the Moto are not commonly seen within Rokugan. They are an extremely proud family who refuse to compromise their ways, often delighting in the discomfort they cause. To them, institutions are to be challenged and opinions openly voiced, which can ruffle more than a few feathers.

Strength is the greatest of the Moto's values, and much of their culture is an expression of this. They interpret the virtue of strength as threefold: Strength of Body, which is hardiness and physical prowess; Strength of Mind, which is cunning and sound reason; and Strength of Heart, which is fearlessness and right action. Hesitation is akin to weakness, and strength exists only if it is demonstrated. This is why the Moto do not believe in half measures. A Moto only attempts something if they intend to give it their all. This is the source of the Moto's reputation, not only for recklessness but also for incredible feats. And while others may respect what the Moto have accomplished while jeering at who they are, the Moto draw no distinctions between the two.

While few outside the Unicorn have ever actually met a Moto, most see the Moto as barbarians, invasive and destructive brutes who are Rokugani only in name. This is the reputation that precedes them based on rumors and documented traditions that Rokugani elite find odious, a few unfortunate diplomatic disasters, and their relentless battle prowess. The Moto are difficult for most Rokugani to comprehend; they smile when they are threatened and laugh as they fight. Others regard this as a loss of reason, but it is merely a way the Moto express their values: strength of resolve and being at peace with a glorious death. Those who take the time to understand them may see that these are values the Moto share with other Great Clan samurai.

### Culture and Traditions

The Moto ways are most similar to those of the Ujik tribes of the steppes, but they have also developed unique traditions influenced by the other Unicorn families. Of all the cultures of the Empire, theirs is the most divergent, as they have no use for the “soft trappings” of the other clans. They believe stationary life dulls the senses and makes one soft, while the nomadic lifestyle they fully embrace refines the spirit into an unbreakable force. They like to say that the ideal life is one spent entirely on horseback, where one’s feet never touch the ground. Offered a chance to live as the rest of the Empire, they rejected such comforts for the harsh deserts and steppes. Their people most often become boisterous warriors, continually seeking the next battle.

The Moto disregard the rigid social conventions of Rokugan. They live communally in large families called ordu, hunting game and dwelling in mobile villages also called ordu.

This alone puts them at odds with many of the Empire's traditions, which regard the consumption of red meat and constant nomadic living as unsavory and “dirty.” Even more eyebrow-raising is the Moto's general disregard of Bushidō. They are too practical to follow such a restrictive code, seeing it as a rationalization of what they already know: “The weak are meat, and the strong eat.”

Even so, the Moto know they are strongest when united. Moto young are culturally obligated to care for those of a venerable age. A disagreement between family members must be immediately settled, if not by talk, then through a contest or consultation with an elder. Once resolved, the disagreement must be set aside. Carrying family grudges is colloquially called carrying the dead, and some believe it attracts the disfavor of the Lords of Death.

The Moto care little for the perception of others and are extremely proud of their heritage. While other Unicorn samurai at least try to adapt to local customs, the Moto are less likely to alter their behavior, even when visiting another clan's territory. They have garnered a reputation for testing a lord's tolerance. On the other hand, mentioning one's admiration for a specific piece of Moto clothing or art is a guaranteed way to ensure the delivery of several pieces just like it, hand delivered all the way from Khanbulak or beyond! The Moto give things away without second thought, often expecting similar generosity in return.

> **THE SHAME OF THE MOTO**
>
> After the Unicorn returned to Rokugan, they were widely distrusted as outsiders. As the clan sought clout, the Moto looked for feats that would earn the awe and respect of the Empire, as this was how the Ujik people traditionally found their place. So it was that Moto Tsume, the family daimyō, declared that he and his warriors would ride forth and conquer the Shadowlands, ending their threat to Rokugan forever. Against the advice of the Crab Clan, he adorned himself in the relics of his family and led one-third of the Moto's warriors, the Second Wind, beyond the Wall to defeat the Shadowlands menace once and for all.
> Now, Moto Tsume's undying forces ride atop skeletal onikage, terrorizing all who cross their paths. From their stronghold at Fallen Chrysanthemum Lake, the Dark Moto endlessly train and launch relentless raids against the Kaiu Wall. Moto Tsume himself is an immortal wraith enthralled by Jigoku's Taint, a fallen samurai whose ranks grow with each fool he slays. In his grip is Kiro, the ancestral sword of the Ki-Rin, once a symbol of the Shinjo's kinship and trust, now a trophy of the family's hubris.
> The Moto reflect on Tsume's doomed quest to this day. It is to their greatest shame that they have lost the Ki-Rin's ancestral sword, and even more so that they failed to fulfill Tsume's oath to shatter Jigoku's reign. Both the Iuchi Doomhunter and many Moto Avengers seek a way to finally slay Moto Tsume and redeem the family for his prideful mistake.

### THE HIDDEN VALLEY

> **THE HIDDEN VALLEY**
>
> Tucked beneath the horizon of the Burning Sands, the Hidden Valley is a peaceful oasis in the deserts beyond the Pillar of the Sky mountains. The city and its many orchards are mostly underground or below surface level; nothing protrudes above the horizon line. As such, the community is virtually invisible to travelers across the desert, except those who know where to look. The people who live here, the Ganzu, pledged themselves to the Unicorn Clan Champion Shinjo Khulan Khan in 1010. This community is but one of the Unicorn's holdings beyond the Empire's borders, stewarding the Sand Road on the clan's behalf.

### Religious Beliefs

Chief among the Moto traditions is the worship of the Lords of Death. This has been their religious practice since they were one of the Ujik tribes, and it has remained virtually unchanged throughout the centuries. These traditions teach one to be at peace with death and to worship the traditional pantheon, the Ten Lords of Death. These gods are a revolving cadre who each represent one of the ten honorable deaths. Akin to the ancestral spirits worshipped by the Rokugani, the Lords of Death, it is believed, are former mortals who have since ascended to godhood and hold power over the cycles of past and future, and of life and death. This is why the Moto do not shy away from death. They believe that death is among their allies and that if they prove worthy, they might even replace one of the Lords of Death and serve, for a brief time, as a god. The religious branches of Rokugan do not quite know what to make of the Moto's spiritual practices. Because proximity to death is considered spiritually staining and a source of misfortune, Fortunists and priests of the elemental spirits regard the Lords of Death as vengeful spirits to be avoided, if they accept the religious practice at all. The Brotherhood of Shinsei is somewhat more accepting, as Shinsei, too, taught that death was merely a new beginning. Such monks rationalize the Lords of Death as beings caught in a state of worldly transition, lingering until

> **MOTO RUMORS**
>
> "They say Moto Ogödei's youngest son is determined to join the Crab Clan through one of its so-called 'Twenty Goblin Winters.' Anyone can see he is chasing after the fallen lord of the Dark Moto. The fool."
> "Some brash idiot suggested the next Imperial Winter Court be held in Khanbulak. Worse, the Imperials are considering it!… Go ahead and laugh, but if they decide to come here, this exciting place will be just another boring do-nothing city within a decade; just you wait and see!"

One way that Moto culture expresses veneration of fearlessness and strength is through teasing and provocation. Moto tend to slip friendly jibes into daily conversation, never hesitating to humorously goad or to highlight a flaw. The expectation is that such remarks be met in kind: taking offense suggests a lack of courage, not proof of it, and is considered a social misstep. Outsiders balk at how openly a band of Moto verbally cut each other down without any of them seeming to take any of it seriously. A related custom, especially among the young, is daring one another into potentially dangerous action. Accepting such a dare is proof of one's courage, even if the action is never actually attempted. This is because most of these "social dares" are impossible and outlandish—and they are often sarcastic. Of course, actually following through on such dares is a quick way to garner a reputation. Another Moto practice is the Galloping Song, most frequently observed when the family musters especially large forces. In this tradition, the horde starts a simple anthem that quickly spreads throughout the ranks until the entire army is singing in unison. It is often timed so that the thundering of the horses, when galloping in unison, provides the rhythmic beat of each verse. It is used to raise the morale of the horde or, when performed just before a charge, to intimidate enemies with the eerie chant of a foe content with death.

such a time as they can pass into Meido, the Realm of Waiting, where the deceased await judgment. Isawa scholars of the Phoenix attempt to reconcile the Lords of Death as lesser servants of Emma-Ō, the Fortune of Death, or perhaps a few of his judges. Others are quick to point out that the Moto gods are also their blessed ancestors, and that veneration of one's ancestors is expected of all samurai. The Moto are content to allow outsiders to speculate however they wish. What matters to them is not the perceptions of others but being true to themselves.

Very few outside the Unicorn have ever laid eyes on the Moto provinces, for they are not technically even provinces of the Empire. The Moto "lands" are the vast steppe just beyond the western border of Rokugan, stretching to the Pillar of the Sky mountain range. It is a territory they share with other Ujik tribes, treating them as "vassals" for all intents and purposes. However, such fealty is purely theoretical; for the tribes of the steppes, life continues as it always has, no matter which family "claims" to own the land.

Inside the Empire, the Moto claim only a handful of true holdings. The greatest and most well-known is Khanbulak, a massive city that is divided by the Empire's border. There, foreign trade is permitted, and contact with the outside world is carefully controlled. The city of Khanbulak is more thoroughly examined on page 19.
